  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-330635",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/597125714057269b7adf9c0a2a9bbdde9631cf37bc80518281ab4444c3189717.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ we have the following symmetry property for $a>0:$\n\n$$\nP(Z>a)=P(Z<-a)=Φ(-a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z>0.73).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/6d98ddf273e1176278fd9d05da5bbe4c073daba99a7cdfadc39d011ac4acc34c.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z<-0.73).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/28bd59bc3f54997cf4ee9e21de15c3ec3dfe8c5cf6d8194709e9aee2d23b7658.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z>0.73)=P(Z<-0.73)=Φ(-0.73)=0.2327.\n$$",
